        "question_type": "Objective",
        "exam_year": "2010",
        "question_text": "<p>If $2^{x+1} = 8^{2x-3}$, find the value of $x$.</p>",
        "question_image": null,
        "answer_image": null,
        "explanation": "<p>Express both sides with the same base: $8 = 2^3$, so $8^{2x-3} = (2^3)^{2x-3} = 2^{6x-9}$.<br><br>The equation becomes $2^{x+1} = 2^{6x-9}$. Since the bases are equal, the exponents must be equal: $x + 1 = 6x - 9$.<br><br>Solving for $x$: $1 + 9 = 6x - x \\Rightarrow 10 = 5x \\Rightarrow x = 2$.</p>",
